{"title":"Mock AIME 5 2005-2006 Problems/Problem 9","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_AIME_5_2005-2006_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/0/030e19e4e2f1c074127cb50581e8496470c5419f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$13$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> nondistinguishable residents are moving into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> distinct houses in Conformistville, with at least one resident per house. In how many ways can the residents be assigned to these houses such that there is at least one house with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> residents?\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_AIME_5_2005-2006_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since the residents are indistinguishable and no houses are empty we can assume that each house has 1 resident.<br />There are 6 residents left (because we put 7 residents in 7 houses). And the condition regarding no empty houses existing doesn't apply anymore.<br />It follows that one house has only 3 residents(3+1=4) so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/c/0dc654850894882d453fbda46ff91bf1f7c3e880.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{7}{1}=7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"70\" height=\"53\" /> and since we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/7/af71a359c5e7cd6171f43e55b8fbbf1bd7d3ea4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6-3=3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"76\" height=\"15\" /> residents and 6 houses left <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/6/816cf219f6efb2e3418c5382bc6ec866e5a9825d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{6+3-1}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"102\" height=\"55\" />(same method for counting number of the sum of integers so just stars and bars).\nBy MP we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/5/ed5ee5bfac730e101eedea1c626d974b7493a0ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{7}{1}\\binom{8}{3}=7\\times56=392$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"202\" height=\"55\" />.<br />but we're overcounting the number of ways the remaining 6 residents can be distributed because the count for 2 houses having 3 residents is being counted twice(yet again talking about the remaining residents).<br />So to subtract we have to count the ways 2 houses have both 3 residents <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/3/1f3a9f117a17b1ad4bbfb7f3ceb8f6ae31083c20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\binom{7}{2}=21$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"78\" height=\"53\" /> because now just 2 objects are being chosen for 7 houses.,<br />So our final result is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/c/91c97e4cf2c3f190403fdd10684fc6f89bc8b3f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$392-21=\\boxed{371}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"132\" height=\"25\" />\n<br />~solution added by samsianati\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_AIME_5_2005-2006_Problems/Problem_9&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_AIME_5_2005-2006\" title=\"Mock AIME 5 2005-2006\">Mock AIME 5 2005-2006</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_AIME_5_2005-2006_Problems\" title=\"Mock AIME 5 2005-2006 Problems\">Problems</a></b>, <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?t=76847\">Source</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_AIME_5_2005-2006_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"Mock AIME 5 2005-2006 Problems/Problem 8\">Problem 8</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_AIME_5_2005-2006_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"Mock AIME 5 2005-2006 Problems/Problem 10\">Problem 10</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_AIME_5_2005-2006_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"Mock AIME 5 2005-2006 Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_AIME_5_2005-2006_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"Mock AIME 5 2005-2006 Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_AIME_5_2005-2006_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"Mock AIME 5 2005-2006 Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_AIME_5_2005-2006_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"Mock AIME 5 2005-2006 Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_AIME_5_2005-2006_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"Mock AIME 5 2005-2006 Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_AIME_5_2005-2006_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"Mock AIME 5 2005-2006 Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b>